A||||36||msweldon||For those of you who doubted, a ~270hp na 3.4 DOHC||||14||
Z||000000||msweldon||05-23-2001||02:47 PM|||| <A HREF="http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/intake/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/intake/index.html</A> <P>Too bad he beat me to it, but Micheal Smith from CA did it on a relatively cheap budget and a little know-how.  He just sold the project car with the engine but I plan on taking his work and continuing on with it, by hopefully having a local speed shop, <A HREF="http://www.Engineered.net," TARGET=_blank>www.Engineered.net,</A>  fabricate a "more" tuned intake system than seen here.  And then move on to some custom cams.<P>Anyway take a look, he's got the whole process pretty well documented.||||reg||14
Z||000001||Formula88||05-23-2001||03:14 PM||||COOL!  Keep us posted on your continuing development.  I always did think the 3.4DOHC fit the personality of the Fiero the best.  If I could get a NA 3.4DOHC to put out the power of a 3800SC, that would be the way to go.  A little soft on the low end would only help clutch/tranny life and help improve launch traction in our little cars.  I'm thinking it's about the best of both worlds.||||reg||10
Z||000002||fropuff||05-23-2001||03:15 PM||||How much did he spend on the whole thing?  If I did those things when I get a 3.4L pushrod, and do a turbo as well, just think of the possibilities.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||msweldon||05-23-2001||03:35 PM||||He fabricated the intake out of scrap aluminum from a boneyard.  His hobby is welding, and he's not an expert at it either.<P>Hopefully if I can get a tuned and standardized intake manifold frabricated from Engineered I'll see if they'd be willing to make more for everyone else wishing to take this path, given that there is enough interest in it.<P>Micheal is currently getting a 3rd gen Rx7, twin seq. turbo twin-13b rotary, and he says his modified DOHC is dead even in performance..  pretty impressive....a poor mans NSX....hehehehe  boy-howdy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||msweldon||05-23-2001||03:40 PM||||fropuff,<P>I'm no automotive engineer, but I don't believe a manifold and such would yield the same results for a 3.4pr.  The DOHC heads allow really high flow rates in comparison to a 2 valve/cylinder engine, which makes this modification so effective.  That DOHC has freakin unbelievable heads for its day, even impressive for today's standards.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wannabe||05-23-2001||04:00 PM||||Take a look at the RPM where the power output was achieved. You may be able to get the same power out of a 3.4pr, but you will have to build it to run that high. Nice project. Keep in mind that the 3.4 DOHC he used was from a '96 car. The '96 up DOHC's had bigger ports, runners and and TB as well as a different indake manifold design. You could even take the project further and hook that high revving engine up to an Isuzu 5spd with the 4.11 fd gearset that is available to make better use of the high rpm capability.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Black88GT||05-23-2001||08:28 PM||||I'm shooting for 250 Hp in my 3.4 DOHC project.  I figure it shouldn't be too out of reach.  I am going to try and build it to redline around 8 grand or so.  <P>Thats a cool site though, learned a lot from it.   Happen to have that guys email address?  I didn't see it on the page.<P>thanks.||||reg||14
Z||000007||msweldon||05-24-2001||12:42 AM||||I'll see if he doesn't mind, he's getting out of the fiero arena however.  Fire some of your questions toward me, I was in constant communication with him during his project, I "may" be able to answer some of your questions OR join the TDC mailing list, the link is given on the web site.<P>marc||||reg||1
